---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import {PAYROLL} from '../data/payroll-2026.mjs';
import SourceStatus from '../components/SourceStatus.astro';
---
<Base title="Calculation methodology" description="The actual payroll, monthly savings, mortgage, emergency-fund and deposit models used by Howloop, with rounding, worked examples and important exclusions.">
 <div class="hl-wrap hl-prose-wrap"><article class="hl-prose">
  <h1>How we calculate</h1>
  <p>All amounts are Australian dollars. These tools run mathematical scenarios in your browser. They do not predict rates, establish product eligibility, decide tax deductibility or recommend a financial product.</p>
  <h2 id="salary">Take-home pay: regular PAYG withholding</h2>
  <p>The <a href="/calculators/salary/">salary calculator</a> uses the {PAYROLL.year} ATO regular-pay withholding schedules, rather than simply dividing an annual tax bill. It converts weekly, fortnightly or monthly pay to weekly equivalent earnings and applies the published coefficients and payroll rounding. Weekly and fortnightly results can differ slightly from a monthly-derived annual estimate.</p>
  <p>Employer super is separate from cash pay. The standard model uses {PAYROLL.superRate*100}% of assumed qualifying earnings, capped at {PAYROLL.superEarningsCap.toLocaleString('en-AU')} dollars annually. For a package including super below the cap, cash salary = package ÷ 1.12. For example, an $89,600 package contains $80,000 gross cash salary and $9,600 employer super. Custom employer contributions can be entered separately.</p>
  <p>The annual estimate is twelve monthly pays. HELP withholding uses the separate published schedule if selected. Standard Medicare treatment is included in withholding; final annual liability can differ. The tool excludes foreign residents, working holiday makers, missing TFNs, Medicare exemptions and family adjustments, Medicare levy surcharge, salary sacrifice, irregular bonuses, termination payments and individual withholding variations.</p>
  <ul><li><a href={PAYROLL.source}>ATO Withholding Schedules Instrument 2026, schedules 1 and 8</a></li><li><a href={PAYROLL.superSource}>ATO super guarantee rates and contribution base</a></li></ul>
  <SourceStatus checked={PAYROLL.checked} days={30}/>
  <h2 id="savings">Savings and compound interest</h2>
  <p>For a constant annual rate, monthly rate r = annual percentage ÷ 1,200. Each month: new balance = previous balance × (1 + r) + monthly contribution. Contributions are added at the end of the month. Weekly and fortnightly deposits are converted using 52 and 26 payments per year, divided by twelve. Years are rounded to whole months.</p>
  <p>At a hypothetical 12% annual rate, $1,000 with no deposits earns $10 in month one and $10.10 in month two. The balance after twelve months is about $1,126.83. The high example rate makes the arithmetic easy to inspect; it is not an offer.</p>
  <p>The savings-goal tool solves the same recurrence for the required monthly contribution. At 0%, it uses (target − starting balance) ÷ months, floored at zero. Values keep numerical precision during calculation and are rounded for display. These models exclude tax, fees, inflation, withdrawals and future rate changes. Real bank daily calculations may differ.</p>
  <p><a href="https://moneysmart.gov.au/saving/compound-interest">ASIC Moneysmart: compound interest</a> · <a href="/learn/how-compound-interest-works/">Our worked guide</a></p>
  <h2 id="mortgage">Mortgage and offset: a monthly approximation</h2>
  <p>For principal P, monthly rate r and n monthly payments, the scheduled repayment is P × r ÷ [1 − (1 + r)<sup>−n</sup>]. At 0%, the payment is P ÷ n. Each month, modelled interest is max(0, remaining balance − offset) × r. The scheduled payment plus any extra repayment is then applied, capped to the remaining balance and interest.</p>
  <p>The comparison holds the rate and offset balance constant and excludes fees, redraw rules, repayment-date changes and lender-specific rounding. It uses monthly interest, not an exact daily lender ledger. An offset does not reduce the scheduled repayment in this model; it can reduce the simulated interest and term. Savings interest and owner-occupied loan interest saved have different tax treatment; the mortgage calculator does not assess your tax position.</p>
  <p><a href="https://moneysmart.gov.au/home-loans/mortgage-offset-accounts">ASIC Moneysmart: mortgage offset accounts</a></p>
  <h2 id="other-tools">Emergency funds, home deposits and cash flow</h2>
  <p>Emergency-fund target = entered essential monthly expenses × selected buffer months. The gap is the target minus existing savings, floored at zero. Months to close that gap are rounded up; interest is excluded. The selected buffer is your scenario, not a personalised recommendation.</p>
  <p>Loan-to-value ratio = loan ÷ property value × 100. Purchase costs reduce the cash available for a deposit. The result does not establish lender approval, insurance pricing or grant eligibility. See the <a href="/calculators/lvr/">deposit calculator</a> for its inputs and examples.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/tools/cash-flow/">13-week planner</a> uses date-only payment schedules and integer cents across 91 days. Income is added and bills subtracted on their scheduled dates. It reports end-of-day balances; it cannot predict the order of bank transactions within a day. The fixed worked example remains separate from your editable plan.</p>
  <h2 id="bank-data">Dated bank data</h2>
  <p>CDR identifies participating banking brands; rates and terms come from provider product endpoints. Automatically modelled rates require supported VARIABLE rows, unambiguous conditions and supported dollar tiers. Bonus and introductory components are shown separately; only supported explicit scenarios may be compared. Ambiguous total-rate wording, unmodelled expiry periods and unsupported balance caps are withheld rather than guessed.</p>
  <p>Each snapshot has its own retrieval date. Failed, missing, future or older-than-seven-day snapshots are unavailable for automatic calculations. A scheduled refresh is an attempt, not a promise of fresh or complete coverage. Check fees, eligibility and the provider’s current terms before opening an account.</p>
  <p><a href="/banks/">Inspect the bank snapshots</a> · <a href="/updates/">Corrections and data updates</a> · <a href="/contact/">Report a result</a></p>
 </article></div>
</Base>
